  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe strong ridging that has sat entrenched across the Southeast\nthis week weakens and begins shifting back west over the Plains\nin response to low pressure deepening off the New England and\neastern Canada Atlantic coast. This doesn't change the pattern\nover the local area much as we remain locked between these\nfeatures under deep-layer northwest flow.\n\nThunderstorms currently forming over the upper Great Lakes are \nforecast to organize into a line/MCS and advance southeast\nacross the Great Lakes overnight through Friday morning and \ninto the local area early Friday afternoon, according to timing\nin the latest convective-allowing model runs. If that timing \nends up verifying, it'll coincide with peak heating/instability \nand storms will therefore carry another risk for damaging wind \ngusts and hail (highlighted by another Storm Prediction Center\nslight risk of severe weather -- level 2 out of 5 -- for the \nlocal area on Friday).\n\nAs the first wave of storms moves through the area early Friday\nafternoon, another wave is progged to fire upstream over the \nMichigan LP and central Great Lakes. That wave is then forecast \nto move southeast towards the local area late Friday night. \nWhile surface-based instability will be limited due to the loss \nof daytime heating, model soundings suggest plenty of elevated \ninstability and even some dry air aloft capable of supporting a\ncontinued damaging wind gust and hail threat. There is some \nuncertainty regarding how far east the second round makes it, as\nthe ridge should be retreating to the west at that point and \ndeep-layer northwest flow becomes a bit more NNWerly. Some CAMs\nsuggest that causes the Friday night convection to dive south \nacross central Ohio, staying mostly to our west. Will need to \nsee how the day evolves before gaining confidence in that track \nand where impacts may be felt.\n\nBy the time storms depart to our south Saturday morning, a cold\nfront should begin making its way into the area from the north.\nSome additional showers and storms could fire along the front\nduring the afternoon, mainly along/south of I-70. Behind the\nfront, drier air and more seasonable temperatures return through\nthe rest of the weekend and into early next week.",
